In article <xeno.714943720@pv34ad.vincent.iastate.edu> xeno@iastate.edu (Gary L Snethen) writes:
>Hello...
>
>   Let's say I am interested in putting nethack up on my UNIX system for
>all users to share and play.  This is a secure system and I can't allow
>world write access to the games directory, for fear someone will either
>wreak havoc or store their dirty GIFs there... ;)
>
>   Is this possible?  How do I handle the bones files so that people can
>stumble across each others' piles?  nethack tries to erase the bones files,
>correct?  It can't if the person has no write access...
>
>How is this typically handled by games administrators?  What are my options?
>
>Thanks,
>
>   Xeno (Gary) Snethen

	Luckily, it's really easy.  What you need to do is set the
executable as SUID.  The command should be something like "chmod u+s
nethack" depending on your system.  This causes all files accessed or generated
by the program to think that the user is you.  Of course, the executable
and its directory must be publicly readable.
